<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conradin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1252">1252</a>–<a href="/years/1268">1268</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Conrad III of Jerusalem, Conrad V of Swabia · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Conradin was the last legitimate member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen dynasty</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> and claimant to the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. At fifteen he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to recover Sicily from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>, but was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tagliacozzo">Battle of Tagliacozzo</a> in 1268. Captured while fleeing, he was beheaded in the market square of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 29 October 1268.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conradin.jpg" alt="Conradin" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konradin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Pope Clement IV invested Charles of Anjou, brother of Louis IX of France, with the kingdom; in February 1266 Charles defeated and killed King Manfred at the Battle of Benevento and in 1268 defeated the last Hohenstaufen heir, <b>Conradin</b>, at the Battle of Tagliacozzo, beheading him in Naples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Manfred, Frederick’s son, was killed at the Battle of Benevento in 1266 by Charles of Anjou, who then defeated <b>Conradin</b> at Tagliacozzo in 1268.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Using a hidden reserve, Charles I of Anjou defeated <b>Conradin</b>, the sixteen-year-old last heir of the Hohenstaufen, who had come to reclaim the Kingdom of Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-23">23 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Conradin</b>, the last legitimate Hohenstaufen, who at fifteen had invaded Italy to recover Sicily from Charles of Anjou, was beheaded in the market square of Naples after his defeat at Tagliacozzo and capture while fleeing.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-29">29 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of the Staufer, the last duke, <b>Conradin</b>, was beheaded at Naples in 1268.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-swabia">Duchy of Swabia</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis II made Munich a ducal capital and acquired Hohenstaufen lands after <b>Conradin's</b> execution in 1268.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-upper-bavaria">Duchy of Upper Bavaria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Burgruine Wolfstein, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.58,12.26&amp;year=1262" aria-label="Burgruine Wolfstein on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4834 466 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Conradin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5284 736h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5284 736h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.58,12.26&amp;year=1262">What was here in 1262? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
